metal: give the transposed GEMV an occupancy floor

`gemv_axbpy` picks the transposed-matrix kernel's tile from two thresholds:

    if (in_vector_len >= 8192 && out_vector_len >= 2048) { sm = 4; sn = 8; }
    else                                                 { sm = 8; sn = 4; }
    bn = out_vector_len >= 2048 ? 16 : out_vector_len >= 512 ? 4 : 2;
    n_out_per_tgp = bn * sn * tn;
    n_tgp         = ceil(out_vector_len / n_out_per_tgp);   // == the grid

One threadgroup covers `bn * sn * tn` outputs and loops over the whole
contracted axis, so every widening of the tile DIVIDES the number of
threadgroups the dispatch launches.  The widest choice (sn = 8 with bn = 16)
covers 512 outputs -- and a 5376-wide projection is then eleven threadgroups
for an 84 MB read.  Most of the GPU is idle and the dispatch runs at a
fraction of memory bandwidth.

Measured on an M5 Max, bfloat16, M = 1, best of 30, `x @ w` with w row-major
[K, N]:

    K = 8192, N = 5376   11 threadgroups   0.709 ms
    K = 8191, N = 5376   21 threadgroups   0.336 ms

One element less on the contracted axis, one branch different, 2.1x.  The
same cliff shows up as a storage-orientation effect, because `[N, K]` read
through `.T` takes the OTHER kernel entirely:

    contraction              row-major [K,N]   [N,K] read .T
    K=8192  -> N=5376              0.709            0.346
    K=21504 -> N=5376              0.990            0.585
    K=5376  -> N=8192  (below)     0.332            0.305
    K=5376  -> N=43008 (below)     1.025            0.982

Only the two dispatches in the `>= 8192` branch are affected, which is what
made this look like a layout problem rather than a tiling one.

The fix narrows the simdgroup back (sn 8 -> 4, sm 4 -> 8; the product stays
32) while the grid would be under 32 threadgroups.  sn = 4 is the shape every
`in_vector_len < 8192` dispatch already uses, so this only ever restores a
well-trodden kernel; nothing new is compiled and no other shape moves.

After, through a PJRT plugin on the same machine (3 runs each, medians, the
`as-is` = row-major arm):

    contraction              before   after   [N,K].T reference
    31B attn_vec K=8192      0.605    0.476   0.340
    31B down    K=21504      1.012    0.674   0.598
    31B q_proj  K=5376       0.595    0.576   0.578   (untouched, as designed)
    31B gating  K=5376       1.058    1.047   0.985   (untouched)
    12B attn_vec K=4096      0.235    0.238   0.187   (untouched)
    12B down    K=15360      1.013    0.524   0.365

Whole-model, gemma-4-12B-it bf16 decode, 128 greedy tokens, one process per
cell, against a control built from the same tree with this hunk reverted:
74.7 -> 63.9 ms/token, token stream byte-identical.

Signed-off-by: (prepared locally; not pushed)
---
diff --git a/mlx/backend/metal/matmul.cpp b/mlx/backend/metal/matmul.cpp
--- a/mlx/backend/metal/matmul.cpp
+++ b/mlx/backend/metal/matmul.cpp
@@ -1107,6 +1107,28 @@ void gemv_axbpy(

     // Specialized kernel for very small outputs
     tn = out_vector_len < tn ? 1 : tn;
+
+    // Occupancy floor.  One threadgroup covers bn * sn * tn outputs, so the
+    // grid is out_vector_len divided by that: every widening of the tile
+    // divides the number of threadgroups the dispatch launches.  The widest
+    // choice here (sn = 8 with bn = 16) covers 512 outputs, which on a
+    // 5376-wide projection is ELEVEN threadgroups for an 84 MB read -- most
+    // of the GPU idle, and the dispatch running at a fraction of memory
+    // bandwidth.  Narrow the simdgroup back until the grid can fill the
+    // machine; sn = 4 is the shape every in_vector_len < 8192 dispatch
+    // already uses, so this only ever restores a well-trodden kernel.
+    //
+    // Measured on an M5 Max, bfloat16, M = 1, best of 30: at K = 8192,
+    // N = 5376 the wide tile gives 11 threadgroups and 0.709 ms; one element
+    // less (K = 8191) takes the narrow branch, gives 21, and costs 0.336 ms.
+    // A 2.1x cliff either side of a threshold that is about K alone.
+    constexpr int min_threadgroups = 32;
+    while (sn > 4 &&
+           (out_vector_len + bn * sn * tn - 1) / (bn * sn * tn) <
+               min_threadgroups) {
+      sn /= 2;
+      sm *= 2;
+    }

     n_out_per_tgp = bn * sn * tn;
     kname << "gemv_t_" << type_to_name(out);
